.Dd October 22, 2026
.Dt XFT_RT 3
.Os xft
.Sh NAME
.Nm xft_get_kernel_ptrs ,
.Nm xft_get_rt ,
.Nm xft_kernel_ptrs_destroy ,
.Nm xft_dyn_cpu_select ,
.Nm xft_exit_process ,
.Nm xft_main
.Nd program entry point and runtime helpers
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In rt.h
.In x86_64.h
.Ft t_kernel_ptrs
.Fn xft_get_kernel_ptrs "const t_any *sp"
.Ft t_xft_rt
.Fn xft_get_rt "const t_any *sp"
.Ft void
.Fn xft_kernel_ptrs_destroy "t_kernel_ptrs *kp"
.Ft t_xft_arch
.Fn xft_dyn_cpu_select void
.Ft void
.Fn xft_exit_process "t_i32a code"
.Ft void
.Fn xft_main "const t_any *restrict sp"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.In rt.h
declares the types that carry the process arguments and the entry points
that hand them to
xft
programs.
On Linux and Windows a program written against
xft
defines
.Fn xft_main
instead of
.Fn main ;
the library supplies the real entry point and calls
.Fn xft_main
with a pointer to the initial stack.
.Ss Types
.Bd -literal -offset indent
typedef t_u8   t_nat_str;
typedef const t_nat_str *__restrict__ *__restrict__ t_rt_arr;

typedef struct s_kernel_ptrs {
	t_size              argc;
	t_rt_arr            argv;
	t_rt_arr            envp;   /* const t_nat_str * on Windows */
} t_kernel_ptrs;

typedef struct s_hwcaps {
	t_u64a              hwcap;
	t_u64a              hwcap2;
} t_hwcaps;

typedef struct s_xft_rt {
	t_kernel_ptrs       kp;
} t_xft_rt;

typedef struct s_xft_arch {
	bool                has_128_vec;
	bool                has_256_vec;
	bool                has_512_vec;
	t_x86_ver           x86_64_ver;
} t_xft_arch;

typedef enum e_x86_ver {
	XFT_X86_NONE,
	XFT_X86_V1,
	XFT_X86_V2,
	XFT_X86_V3,
	XFT_X86_V4,
} t_x86_ver;
.Ed
.Pp
.Vt t_nat_str
is the character type of the arguments and the environment.
.Vt t_kernel_ptrs
holds the argument count, the argument vector and the environment.
.Vt t_hwcaps
is reserved for the auxiliary vector's hardware capability words; it is
declared but not populated.
.Vt t_xft_rt
wraps
.Vt t_kernel_ptrs
so that more runtime state can be added without changing the
.Fn xft_main
signature.
.Vt t_xft_arch
reports which vector widths the running CPU and operating system support,
and on x86_64 the psABI microarchitecture level they reach;
.Vt t_x86_ver ,
from
.In x86_64.h ,
names those levels in increasing order, so they compare with
.Ql >= .
See
.Sx CPU detection .
.Ss Entry point
.Fn xft_main
is the function a program defines.
It is declared
.Ql nonnull
and
.Ql noreturn
and must end with
.Fn xft_exit_process .
.Pp
.Fn xft_exit_process
ends the whole process with exit status
.Fa code :
.Xr exit_group 2
on Linux and
.Ql ExitProcess
on Windows.
With
.Dv XFT_REQUIRE_LIBC
no
.Xr atexit 3
handlers run and no stdio buffers are flushed.
.Fa sp
points to the initial stack as it was on entry to the process; it is not a
.Vt t_kernel_ptrs .
.Pp
The library provides the real entry point,
.Fn _start ,
for every supported platform when
.Dv XFT_NO_RT
is not defined.
On Linux it does not call into libc:
.Bl -tag -width "aarch64"
.It x86_64
Clears
.Va rbp ,
moves
.Va rsp
into
.Va rdi ,
aligns
.Va rsp
to 16 bytes, and calls
.Fn xft_main
with the original stack pointer.
.It aarch64
Clears
.Va x29 ,
passes
.Va sp
in
.Va x0
after aligning it to 16 bytes, and calls
.Fn xft_main
with the unmodified value.
.El
.Pp
On Windows
.Fn _start
passes the frame address of the entry point to
.Fn xft_main .
.Pp
With
.Dv XFT_REQUIRE_LIBC
set, the library instead defines
.Fn main
and forwards to
.Fn xft_main
with the stack pointer reconstructed from the arguments:
on Linux, from the address of
.Fa argv
minus one word, which is where
.Fa argc
lives; on Windows, from the frame address.
.Ss Recovering arguments
.Fn xft_get_kernel_ptrs
fills a
.Vt t_kernel_ptrs :
.Bl -tag -width "Windows"
.It Linux
.Fa sp
is treated as a pointer to an array of machine words
.Pq Vt t_size .
.Fa argc
is at index 0,
.Fa argv
starts at index 1, and
.Fa envp
follows the terminating
.Dv nullptr
of
.Fa argv .
No allocation is performed and the returned pointers alias the process
stack.
.It Windows
.Fa sp
is ignored.
.Fa argv
is the command line split by
.Ql CommandLineToArgvW ,
in UTF-8.
.Fa envp
is the block returned by
.Ql GetEnvironmentStringsA .
Both are allocated and must be released with
.Fn xft_kernel_ptrs_destroy .
The process exits with status 1 if either cannot be obtained.
.El
.Pp
.Fn xft_get_rt
calls
.Fn xft_get_kernel_ptrs
and wraps the result in a
.Vt t_xft_rt .
.Pp
.Fn xft_kernel_ptrs_destroy
releases the storage owned by
.Fa kp
and zeroes it.
On Linux it does nothing.
.Ss CPU detection
.Fn xft_dyn_cpu_select
queries the running CPU and returns which vector widths are usable:
.Bl -tag -width "aarch64"
.It x86_64
.Fa x86_64_ver
is the highest x86-64 psABI level the CPU and operating system fully
support, each level including the previous ones:
.Bl -tag -width "XFT_X86_NONE"
.It Dv XFT_X86_NONE
CPUID is unavailable or the CPU lacks SSE2.
.It Dv XFT_X86_V1
SSE2.
.It Dv XFT_X86_V2
SSE3, SSSE3, SSE4.1, SSE4.2, POPCNT, CMPXCHG16B and LAHF/SAHF.
.It Dv XFT_X86_V3
AVX, AVX2, BMI1, BMI2, F16C, FMA, LZCNT, MOVBE and OSXSAVE, with the SSE
and AVX state enabled in XCR0.
.It Dv XFT_X86_V4
AVX512F, AVX512BW, AVX512CD, AVX512DQ and AVX512VL, with the opmask and
ZMM state enabled in XCR0.
.El
.Pp
The widths follow the level, so that a build made with
.Fl march Ns = Ns Cm x86-64-v Ns Ar N
only runs where it is reported usable:
.Fa has_128_vec
is set from
.Dv XFT_X86_V1 ,
.Fa has_256_vec
from
.Dv XFT_X86_V3
and
.Fa has_512_vec
from
.Dv XFT_X86_V4 .
AVX512VBMI2 is not part of any level and is not reported; see
.Xr xft_bmi 3 .
.It aarch64
.Fa has_128_vec
is always set, since NEON is mandatory; the wider fields are never set and
.Fa x86_64_ver
is
.Dv XFT_X86_NONE .
.El
.Pp
On any other architecture every field is false.
.Pp
The detection is meant for release builds, but it only reports; it does
not switch anything inside the library.
Every vector path in
xft
is selected by the preprocessor from the target ISA macros at compile time,
following
.Fl Dcpu
(see
.Xr xft 7 ) ,
so one build contains exactly one code path for each routine.
A program that has to run on CPUs of different generations should instead
ship one executable per target, each built with its own
.Fl Dcpu
and fully specialized for it, plus a small launcher that calls
.Fn xft_dyn_cpu_select
and executes the matching one.
xft
executables are small enough that bundling several of them costs little.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn xft_get_kernel_ptrs
and
.Fn xft_get_rt
return the filled structure.
.Fn xft_kernel_ptrs_destroy
returns nothing.
.Fn xft_dyn_cpu_select
returns the detected
.Vt t_xft_arch ,
with every field false, and
.Fa x86_64_ver
.Dv XFT_X86_NONE ,
if CPUID is unavailable.
.Fn xft_main
and
.Fn xft_exit_process
do not return.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Print the arguments, one per line, then exit successfully.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
#include <xft.h>

void	xft_main(const t_any *__restrict__ const sp)
{
	t_kernel_ptrs	kp;
	t_writer		w;
	t_u8			buf[BUFSIZE];
	t_size			i;

	kp = xft_get_kernel_ptrs(sp);
	w = xft_get_fs_writer(xft_fatptr(buf, sizeof(buf)), xft_get_stdout());
	i = 0;
	while (i < kp.argc)
	{
		xft_writer_write(&w, xft_fatptr((t_u8 *)kp.argv[i],
				xft_strlen((const char *)kp.argv[i])));
		xft_writer_write(&w, xft_fatptr((t_u8 *)"\en", 1));
		++i;
	}
	xft_writer_flush(&w);
	xft_kernel_ptrs_destroy(&kp);
	xft_exit_process(0);
}
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr xft_io 3 ,
.Xr xft_syscalls 3 ,
.Xr xft 7
.Sh CAVEATS
.Fn xft_get_kernel_ptrs
on Linux reads the stack blindly.
The layout it assumes is the one the kernel passes to a static ELF
executable; a process started by any other mechanism, or one whose stack has
been rearranged before
.Fn xft_main ,
yields garbage in
.Fa argc ,
.Fa argv
or
.Fa envp .
.Pp
.Vt t_hwcaps
is never filled.
Programs that need the auxiliary vector's hardware capabilities must read
them from
.Fa sp
themselves.
